<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.9.3: Writing and Rearranging Equations in Two Variables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00208</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.9.3: Writing and Rearranging Equations in Two Variables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>de0ad1f3-c51a-463f-a972-9382df74400b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>The Department of Streets of a city has a budget of $1,962,800 for resurfacing roads and hiring additional
  workers this year.</para>
      <para>The cost of resurfacing a mile of 2-lane road is estimated at $84,000. The average starting salary of a
  worker in the department is $36,000 a year. </para>
      <figure>
        <media alt="Construction workers laying asphalt.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_11_06_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <!--Q1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an equation that represents the relationship between the miles of 2-lane roads the department
        could resurface, \( m \), and the number of new workers it could
        hire, \( p \), if it spends the entire budget.
      </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( 84,000m + 36,000p = 1,962,800 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q2-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Use the equation you wrote in the first question to solve for \(p\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(p=\frac{1,962,800-84,000m}{36,000}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain what the solution to the equation in question 2 represents in this situation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>It represents the number of new workers the department could hire if it resurfaces \(m\)
      miles of roads and spends its entire budget.
    </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Use the equation you wrote in the first question to solve for \(m\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(m=\frac{1,962,800-36,000p}{84,000}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q5-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain what the solution to the equation in question 4 represents in this situation. </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>It represents the miles of roads the department could resurface if it spends the entire
      budget and hires \(p\) new workers.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise data-content-id="d69975ec-53bc-481f-a953-fe17d426e4c1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The city is planning to hire 6 new workers and use its entire budget. Which equation should be used to find
          how
          many miles of 2-lane roads it could resurface? </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(m=\frac{1,962,800-36,000p}{84,000}\)</para>
            <para>\(84,000m + 36,000p=1,962,800\)</para>
            <para>\(p=\frac{1,962,800-84,000m}{36,000}\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(m=\frac{1,962,800-36,000p}{84,000}\)</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is \(m=\frac{1,962,800-36,000p}{84,000}\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Q7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Substitute 6 for \(p\) in the expression that is equal to \(m\) to find the miles of roads
      that could be resurfaced.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--END QUESTION.-->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise data-content-id="a22b13f2-34d9-490c-9de8-70cfb48cec64" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>To the nearest hundredth, how many miles of 2-lane roads could the city resurface if it hires 6 new workers?
        </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 20.8
        \(m=\frac{1,962,800-36,000(6)}{84,000}\)
        \(m=\frac{1,962,800-36,000(6)}{84,000}\)
        \(m=\frac{1,962,800-216,00084,000}{84,000}\) 
        \(m=\frac{1,962,800-216,00084,000}{84,000}\) 
        \(m=20.8\)
      
        \(m=20.8\)
      </para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 20.8
        \(m=\frac{1,962,800-36,000(6)}{84,000}\)
        \(m=\frac{1,962,800-36,000(6)}{84,000}\)
        \(m=\frac{1,962,800-216,00084,000}{84,000}\) 
        \(m=\frac{1,962,800-216,00084,000}{84,000}\) 
        \(m=20.8\)
      
        \(m=20.8\)
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
